1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,271
Lucy, we really need to get working on this marketing assignment.

2
00:00:03,271 --> 00:00:07,600
We've only got five weeks left to the end of term to design it

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,110
carry it out, and then write up the results.

4
00:00:10,110 --> 00:00:12,420
Sure. Well, let's get started right now

5
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,340
Let's go over the instructions. What exactly do we have to do?

6
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:20,881
Well, it says here we have to look at one area of the entertainment industry.

7
00:00:20,881 --> 00:00:23,000
There's a list of the different types.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,740
What are they?

9
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:27,720
Music, cinema, theatre, sport, and eating out.

10
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:28,710
Is that all?

11
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:29,760
Looks like it.

12
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,890
So we choose one branch of the industry

13
00:00:31,890 --> 00:00:36,150
and then we look at how two different groups of people use it? Is that right?

14
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:36,600
Yes.

15
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,150
And do we have to use any particular method to get our data?

16
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:42,340
Can we mail out a questionnaire

17
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:46,050
or do face-to-face interviews, or maybe even observations?

18
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:49,000
Well actually, it looks like we don't have a choice

19
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,100
We have to do telephone interviews.

20
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:56,160
OK, so at least we don't have to waste time deciding between the different methods.

21
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:00,940
Yeah, that's right. Oh, and the other requirement is the number of interviewees.

22
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:04,200
Not too many, I hope. Ten? Twenty?

23
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,780
Well, we have to do two groups, remember

24
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:11,150
and it looks like we have to interview fifteen for each group.

25
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:15,840
That's thirty altogether then. It's going to take ages.

26
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:18,300
Yes, but remember we're working on this together

27
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:20,530
so we'll only have to do fifteen each.

28
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:23,510
OK, so those are all the requirements?

29
00:01:23,510 --> 00:01:25,430
Yes, looks like it.

30
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:28,470
So, first, which area are we going to choose?

31
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:33,200
My preference would be cinema, since that's where I spend most of my money.

32
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,062
Hmm, I don't think that's such a good idea.

33
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:39,559
I don't think there are huge differences in the market there.

34
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:42,840
I mean you get young and old, male and female

35
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,600
rich and poor all going to the same movies.

36
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,650
Yeah, maybe you're right. Let's make it music then.

37
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:53,920
Right. So, what two groups will we compare and contrast?

38
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:55,670
Male and female?

39
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:59,344
No. Most of my female friends like the same music as me.

40
00:01:59,344 --> 00:02:03,630
Different age groups would be much more likely to show up differences, I think.

41
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:06,360
Yeah, I suppose you're right again

42
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:10,518
I'll take some notes, shall I? So... Age Groups. Well.

43
00:02:10,518 --> 00:02:15,660
What do you think? Maybe twenty-five or under for one group

44
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:18,698
and forty-five or over for the other group?

45
00:02:18,698 --> 00:02:20,850
That should show up differences.

46
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:21,540
Right.

47
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:27,580
OK. Next. How about the kind of music they like-let's give them some choices

48
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:29,250
and then we can just tick boxes.

49
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:35,830
OK. Let's have pop, jazz, folk, easy listening... What else?

50
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:40,120
Well, we should include classical. Some people like it, you know.

51
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:45,120
OK. OK. And then we should have how they listen to music.

52
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:51,440
The medium. Right. Let's include radio, CD, and then I guess there's TV.

53
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,170
What about concerts? You know, in pubs and halls.

54
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:56,600
Oh yeah, we should include live music of course.

55
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:57,700
OK, we're on a roll now

56
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:01,470
Next point could be about where they actually get their music.

57
00:03:01,470 --> 00:03:05,600
You mean like, do they buy it in music shops, or department stores?

58
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,560
Yes, or download it from the Internet.

59
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,760
Right. That could be for recorded music.

60
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,160
Then we need another section for live music. Where do they go for that?

61
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,940
OK. Let's say disco, pub, club, concert hall...

62
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:22,170
Or opera house! And I guess we should include karaoke bars.

63
00:03:22,170 --> 00:03:23,630
Not many of them in this city!

64
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:27,730
OK. We'll leave that out then. So, what's left to do?

65
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:32,240
That's it. Well, now we can make a time-scale for doing it.
